(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Engineering Communication & Ethics"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Engineering Communication & Ethics"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Engineering Communication and Ethics: Assignments with Model Answers\n\nFor writing tasks there is no single correct answer. The model answers show one strong response, and each question has a marking checklist so you can judge your own work against explicit criteria. Do the task first, then mark yourself honestly.\n\n# Assignment 1: Technical Writing (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Clear sentences (12 marks).** Rewrite each sentence so that it is concise, concrete and in the appropriate voice. (2 marks each)\n(a) \"It has been determined by the team that the utilization of a larger heat sink would be beneficial in terms of thermal performance.\"\n(b) \"Due to the fact that the sensor was not calibrated, the readings were, generally speaking, quite inaccurate.\"\n(c) \"The data link layer retransmission timeout parameter configuration was modified.\"\n(d) \"In order to make a determination of the failure cause, an examination of the logs was performed.\"\n(e) \"The new design is much faster and very reliable.\"\n(f) \"Voltage drop measurement was performed on the resistor, and a value of 4.9 was obtained.\"\n\n**Q2. Abstract (12 marks).** Write a 120 to 180 word abstract for the following study. Background: a student team built a low-pass RC filter for an audio sensor. Goal: cut off noise above 3 kHz. Method: they used R = 5.1 kΩ and C = 10 nF, predicted the cutoff frequency, and measured the response with a function generator and oscilloscope from 100 Hz to 100 kHz. Results: predicted fc = 3.12 kHz; measured fc = 3.05 kHz (−3 dB point), a difference of 2.2 percent, within component tolerances (±5 percent); attenuation at 30 kHz was 20.1 dB (predicted 20.0 dB). Conclusion: the design met the specification.\n\n**Q3. Requirements (12 marks).** Each requirement below is defective. Say what is wrong and rewrite it as a necessary, unambiguous, measurable and testable requirement. (3 marks each)\n(a) \"The system should be user-friendly.\"\n(b) \"The response must be fast.\"\n(c) \"The device shall work in harsh environments, etc.\"\n(d) \"The battery shall last a long time and the display shall be bright and also the unit must be cheap.\"\n\n**Q4. Professional email (8 marks).** Write an email from an engineer to a project supervisor reporting that a prototype delivery will slip from 15 May to 29 May because a key component (a motor driver) is delayed by the supplier. Include the impact on the test schedule, a mitigation, and a request for a decision.\n\n**Q5. Uncertainty and units (10 marks).** (a) A resistor is measured with V = 5.00 ± 0.05 V and I = 2.00 ± 0.04 A. Compute R = V/I with its uncertainty, using the quadrature (root-sum-square) rule for relative uncertainties, and state the result with a sensible number of significant figures [5]. (b) Correct the errors in each statement: (i) \"The clock frequency is 16 mhz.\" (ii) \"The sampling rate is 3 ms/s.\" (iii) \"The download rate was 100 MB/s, that is 100 megabits per second.\" (iv) \"The mass was 2.5kg.\" [5]\n\n**Q6. Figures (6 marks).** A student's graph of \"motor speed against supply voltage\" has no axis labels, uses a 3D bar chart, shows five measurements joined by a smooth curve without markers, truncates the y-axis to exaggerate the trend, and uses red and green lines. List the improvements you would make.\n\n**Q7. Citation and plagiarism (10 marks).**\nOriginal passage (from a textbook): \"A feedback controller compares the measured output of a plant to a desired reference and uses the error to adjust the input, so that the output follows the reference despite disturbances.\"\nStudent versions: (A) \"A feedback controller compares the measured output of a plant to a desired reference and uses the error to adjust the input so that the output follows the reference despite disturbances.\" (no citation). (B) \"A feedback controller measures the plant output, compares it to the setpoint, and adjusts the input according to the error so that disturbances are rejected [3].\" (C) \"Feedback control means comparing what the system does to what you want and correcting the difference [3].\"\n(a) Judge each version (acceptable or not) and explain [6]. (b) Write IEEE reference entries for: a book (authors K. Ogata, title *Modern Control Engineering*, 5th edition, Prentice Hall, Upper Saddle River, NJ, USA, 2010); and a standard (IEEE Std 754-2019, *IEEE Standard for Floating-Point Arithmetic*) [4].\n\n## Model Answers and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) \"The team recommends a larger heat sink to improve cooling.\" (Active voice, 10 words versus 24.)\n(b) \"The sensor was not calibrated, so its readings were inaccurate.\" Better still: \"The readings were inaccurate because the sensor was not calibrated; they differed from the reference by 8 percent.\" Cut \"due to the fact that\", \"generally speaking\", \"quite\".\n(c) \"We changed the timeout before the data link layer retransmits.\" Remove the noun stack; say who acted.\n(d) \"We examined the logs to find the cause of the failure.\"\n(e) Replace the vague adjectives with numbers: \"The new design responds in 12 ms instead of 40 ms and failed 0 times in 1,000 test hours.\"\n(f) \"The voltage drop across the resistor was 4.9 V.\" The unit was missing; \"measurement was performed\" is wordy.\nMarking: 1 mark for removing padding or passive voice, 1 mark for precision (units, numbers, clear actor).\n\n**Q2. Model abstract (about 150 words).**\n\"Audio sensors pick up noise above their useful band, which degrades measurements. This study designed and tested a first-order passive RC low-pass filter to remove noise above 3 kHz. A resistor of 5.1 kΩ and a capacitor of 10 nF were chosen, giving a predicted cutoff frequency of 3.12 kHz. The frequency response was measured from 100 Hz to 100 kHz using a function generator and an oscilloscope. The measured −3 dB point was 3.05 kHz, 2.2 percent below the prediction and well within the ±5 percent component tolerance. At 30 kHz the measured attenuation was 20.1 dB, compared with the predicted 20.0 dB. The filter therefore met the design specification, and the simple first-order model predicts its behaviour accurately in this range.\"\nChecklist (1 mark each, 12 total): background/problem; objective; method; component values; predicted value; measured value; quantitative comparison (percent or dB); tolerance interpretation; conclusion; no new information not in the study; correct length and standalone (no citations or figures); clear, tense-consistent writing.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Subjective and untestable. Rewrite: \"A new user shall complete the task of setting a timer in under 60 seconds without help, in at least 9 of 10 trials.\"\n(b) No measurable limit or condition. Rewrite: \"The system shall respond to a valid request within 200 ms for 95 percent of requests at a load of 100 requests per second.\"\n(c) \"Harsh environments\" is undefined and \"etc.\" leaves the scope open. Rewrite: \"The device shall operate at ambient temperatures from −20 °C to +60 °C, relative humidity up to 95 percent non-condensing, and withstand 1.5 m drops onto concrete.\"\n(d) Several requirements joined together, with vague words. Split and quantify: \"The battery shall support at least 48 hours of continuous operation at 25 °C.\" \"The display shall provide a luminance of at least 400 cd/m^2.\" \"The unit cost shall not exceed US$25 at a volume of 10,000.\"\nMarking: 1 mark for naming the defect (vague, untestable, compound, open-ended), 2 for a rewritten measurable requirement with a number, unit and condition.\n\n**Q4. Model email.**\nSubject: Prototype delivery delayed to 29 May: decision needed on test schedule\nDear Dr. Mensah,\nThe prototype delivery will move from 15 May to 29 May. Our supplier has told us that the motor driver ICs (part DRV-8825) will arrive on 22 May instead of 8 May, and assembly and bring-up then take a week.\nImpact: the planned integration test (19 to 23 May) cannot start until 29 May. The final demonstration on 12 June is still achievable if testing is reduced from 10 to 7 days.\nMitigation: (1) we will test the controller code with a development board and the existing driver in the meantime; (2) we have requested a quote for an alternative driver with a 3-day delivery.\nDecision needed by Friday: please confirm either (a) a shortened test plan with the original demo date, or (b) a new demo date of 19 June.\nI will update you as soon as the supplier confirms the shipment.\nKind regards,\nAma Boateng\nChecklist (1 mark each): clear informative subject; main message in the opening; cause stated factually without blaming; revised date; impact quantified; mitigation; explicit request and deadline; professional tone and correct format.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) Step 1: R = 5.00/2.00 = 2.50 Ω.\nStep 2: Relative uncertainties: 0.05/5.00 = 1.0 percent and 0.04/2.00 = 2.0 percent.\nStep 3: Combined: sqrt(1.0^2 + 2.0^2) = sqrt(5) = 2.24 percent. ΔR = 0.0224 · 2.50 = 0.056 Ω.\nStep 4: Report **R = 2.50 ± 0.06 Ω** (uncertainty to one significant figure, the value to the same decimal place) (If the uncertainties were summed linearly the bound would be 3 percent, or 0.075 Ω; quadrature is appropriate for independent random errors.) (Marks: 2 relative uncertainties, 2 combination, 1 presentation.)\n(b) Step 1: (i) **16 MHz** (M for mega; \"mhz\" has the wrong case; the unit is a symbol, so it is capitalised as Hz) (ii) \"ms/s\" means milliseconds per second (a dimensionless ratio of 0.003), not a sampling rate.\nStep 2: A sampling rate of three million samples per second is written **3 MS/s** (capital M for mega; lower-case m means milli).\nStep 3: Prefix case matters (iii) 100 MB/s is 100 megabytes per second, which equals 800 megabits per second (8 bits per byte), so write \"100 MB/s (800 Mb/s)\". (iv) **2.5 kg** (a space between the number and unit) (1 mark each, 1 extra for explaining (ii) and (iii).)\n\n**Q6.** Improvements: label both axes with quantity and unit (for example \"Speed (rpm)\" and \"Supply voltage (V)\"); replace the 3D bars by a 2D scatter plot with markers for the measured points; do not smooth the data with an invented curve, instead fit a justified model (for example a straight line with its equation and R^2) and show it separately from the data; start the y-axis at zero or clearly mark a break; add error bars; choose colour-blind-safe colours and different marker shapes or line styles so that it reads in greyscale; give the figure a number and a caption below it, and refer to it in the text. (1 mark each, up to 6.)\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) **A: not acceptable.** It is a verbatim copy with no quotation marks and no citation: plagiarism. **B: acceptable** only because it restates the idea in a different structure and wording and carries a citation; it is a proper paraphrase (it still depends on the source, so the citation is essential). **C: acceptable.** It is a genuine summary in the student's own words with a citation. (2 marks each: verdict plus reason.)\n(b)\n[1] K. Ogata, *Modern Control Engineering*, 5th ed. Upper Saddle River, NJ, USA: Prentice Hall, 2010.\n[2] *IEEE Standard for Floating-Point Arithmetic*, IEEE Std 754-2019, 2019.\n(2 marks each: correct elements and order, correct punctuation.)\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 2: Standards, Risk and Safety (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Who publishes what? (10 marks)** Match each item to the best description. Items: (1) ISO 9001, (2) IEEE 802.11, (3) IEC 61508, (4) RFC 9293, (5) NFPA 70, (6) ISO 26262, (7) IEC 62304, (8) FCC Part 15, (9) RoHS, (10) IEEE 754. Descriptions: (A) functional safety of road vehicles; (B) floating-point arithmetic; (C) quality management systems; (D) wireless LAN; (E) the US National Electrical Code; (F) functional safety of electrical, electronic and programmable electronic systems; (G) the specification of TCP; (H) medical device software life cycle; (I) US regulation of unintentional radio-frequency emissions; (J) restriction of hazardous substances in electronics.\n\n**Q2. Compliance matrix (14 marks).** A company will sell a battery-powered, Wi-Fi-connected temperature sensor in the EU and in the US. Build a compliance matrix listing at least six applicable regulations or standards (name, what it covers, and what evidence the company must produce).\n\n**Q3. Reading a standard (8 marks).** (a) Explain the difference between \"shall\", \"should\" and \"may\" in a standard [3]. (b) A clause says: \"The enclosure shall withstand a 1.0 m drop onto a concrete surface. NOTE: Rubber bumpers may help.\" Which words are normative and which informative? [2] (c) Why must you cite the edition of a standard? [3]\n\n**Q4. Risk matrix and controls (12 marks).** A lab bench power supply can deliver 10 A and has an exposed terminal. Likelihood and severity are scored 1 to 5, and risk = likelihood × severity. (a) Estimate the risk of an electric shock burn (likelihood 3, severity 3) and of a fire from a short circuit (likelihood 2, severity 5) and rank them [4]. (b) Apply the hierarchy of controls to the fire hazard with at least one control at each of four levels [5]. (c) Re-score the fire risk after controls (likelihood 1, severity 5) and say whether it is acceptable if the threshold is 6 [3].\n\n**Q5. FMEA (8 marks).** Compute the Risk Priority Number RPN = Severity × Occurrence × Detection (each scored 1 to 10, where a higher detection score means harder to detect) for: battery overheating (S 9, O 3, D 4); loose connector (S 6, O 5, D 3); firmware hang (S 7, O 4, D 2). Rank the failure modes and say what you would do first.\n\n**Q6. Reliability (8 marks).** (a) A component has reliability 0.99 over a mission. What is the reliability of three such components in series, and of two in parallel (redundant)? [4] (b) A device has MTBF = 50,000 h with a constant failure rate. Find the failure rate, the probability that it fails within 1,000 h, and its reliability at 1,000 h [4].\n\n**Q7. Safety integrity (6 marks).** Under IEC 61508 (low-demand mode) the Safety Integrity Levels correspond to the average probability of failure on demand (PFDavg): SIL 1: 10^-2 to 10^-1; SIL 2: 10^-3 to 10^-2; SIL 3: 10^-4 to 10^-3; SIL 4: 10^-5 to 10^-4. (a) Which SIL does a PFDavg of 4 × 10^-3 meet? [2] (b) Two independent trip systems in parallel each have PFDavg = 0.05. What is the PFDavg of the combination (assume independence) and which SIL does it reach? [4]\n\n**Q8. Citing standards (4 marks).** Write IEEE reference entries for: (a) IEC 61508-1:2010, *Functional safety of electrical/electronic/programmable electronic safety-related systems: General requirements*; (b) ISO/IEC 27001:2022, *Information security, cybersecurity and privacy protection: Information security management systems: Requirements*.\n\n## Model Answers and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.** (1) ISO 9001 → **C**. (2) IEEE 802.11 → **D**. (3) IEC 61508 → **F**. (4) RFC 9293 → **G**. (5) NFPA 70 → **E**. (6) ISO 26262 → **A**. (7) IEC 62304 → **H**. (8) FCC Part 15 → **I**. (9) RoHS → **J**. (10) IEEE 754 → **B**. (1 mark each.)\n\n**Q2.** A strong matrix includes at least the following (2 marks per row, up to 14):\n- **EU Radio Equipment Directive (RED, 2014/53/EU)** and CE marking: radio efficiency, safety, EMC; evidence: technical file, test reports (ETSI EN 300 328 for 2.4 GHz), declaration of conformity.\n- **EMC (IEC/EN 61000 series, EN 301 489)**: emissions and immunity; evidence: accredited laboratory test report.\n- **FCC Part 15 (US)**: unintentional and intentional radiators; evidence: test report, FCC ID or declaration of conformity.\n- **RoHS (EU 2011/65/EU)**: restricted substances (lead, mercury, cadmium, etc.); evidence: material declarations from suppliers, technical file.\n- **WEEE (2012/19/EU)**: take-back and recycling; evidence: registration and marking (crossed-out bin).\n- **Battery safety (IEC 62133 for lithium cells; UN 38.3 for transport)**: evidence: cell certificates and transport test reports.\n- **Product safety (IEC 62368-1)**: audio/video and information technology equipment; evidence: safety test report.\n- **Cybersecurity** (ETSI EN 303 645 for consumer IoT; RED delegated act): evidence: security assessment.\n- **Wi-Fi Alliance / IEEE 802.11 conformance** (optional but commercially important).\nMarking: correct name and scope (1), evidence (1) for each row; deduct if US and EU are not both covered.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) **shall** = a mandatory requirement (you must comply to claim conformity); **should** = a recommendation (do it unless there is a justified reason not to); **may** = permission (optional). (1 mark each.)\n(b) Normative: \"The enclosure shall withstand a 1.0 m drop onto a concrete surface.\" Informative: the NOTE (\"Rubber bumpers may help\"), which is only an example or hint. (2 marks.)\n(c) Standards are revised and requirements change between editions (limits, test methods, new clauses); compliance is only meaningful relative to a specific edition and date, and regulators or customers may require a specific version. Citing it lets readers verify and ensures that the test report matches the version claimed. (3 marks.)\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Step 1: risk = likelihood × severity. Shock burn: 3 × 3 = **9**.\nStep 2: fire: 2 × 5 = **10**.\nStep 3: ranking: fire (10) is the higher risk, then shock burn (9). (2 marks each: calculations and ranking.)\n(b) Hierarchy of controls: **eliminate** (use a supply with lower current capability, or a design with no exposed live terminal); **substitute** (use a current-limited 2 A supply for tasks that need no more); **engineering controls** (fuse or electronic current limit, thermal cut-out, flame-retardant enclosure, insulated shrouded terminals, residual current device); **administrative** (procedure, labelling, training, inspection schedule); **PPE** (insulating gloves, safety glasses). Award 1 mark for each of four levels correctly illustrated and 1 mark for stating that engineering controls rank above warnings and PPE.\n(c) Step 1: after controls: 1 × 5 = **5**.\nStep 2: compare with the threshold: 5 < 6, so the residual risk is **acceptable (ALARP)** provided the controls are verified, maintained and documented. (1 mark each: calculation, comparison, condition.)\n\n**Q5.**\nStep 1: RPN = S × O × D. Battery overheating = 9 × 3 × 4 = **108**.\nStep 2: loose connector = 6 × 5 × 3 = **90**.\nStep 3: firmware hang = 7 × 4 × 2 = **56**.\nStep 4: ranking: battery overheating (108), loose connector (90), firmware hang (56).\nStep 5: first action: address the battery overheating hazard (it has the highest severity, 9, and the highest RPN) with design changes: a thermal fuse or cut-off, cell protection circuit, and improved detection (temperature monitoring). Note that a single high-severity item should be treated first even if its RPN is not the highest. (2 marks per RPN, 1 ranking, 1 action.)\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Step 1: series (all three must work): R = 0.99³ = 0.99 × 0.99 × 0.99 = **0.9703**.\nStep 2: parallel of two (at least one must work): R = 1 − (1 − 0.99)² = 1 − 0.0001 = **0.9999**. (Redundancy only helps if failures are independent: a common-cause failure defeats it.)\n(b) Step 1: failure rate λ = 1/MTBF = 1/50,000 = **2 × 10^-5 per hour**.\nStep 2: reliability R(t) = e^(-λ t) = e^(-2 × 10^-5 × 1000) = e^(-0.02) = **0.9802**.\nStep 3: probability of failure within 1,000 h = 1 − 0.9802 = **0.0198 (about 2 percent)**.\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) 4 × 10^-3 lies between 10^-3 and 10^-2, so it meets **SIL 2**.\n(b) Step 1: parallel (1-out-of-2) with independent failures: both channels must fail on demand, so PFDavg ≈ 0.05 × 0.05 = **2.5 × 10^-3**.\nStep 2: this lies between 10^-3 and 10^-2: the SIL 2 range. (A real analysis would add a common-cause factor, which can dominate.)\n\n**Q8.**\n[1] *Functional Safety of Electrical/Electronic/Programmable Electronic Safety-Related Systems: General Requirements*, IEC 61508-1:2010, 2010.\n[2] *Information Security, Cybersecurity and Privacy Protection: Information Security Management Systems: Requirements*, ISO/IEC 27001:2022, 2022.\n(2 marks each: title, designation, year.)\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 3: Ethics Case Analysis (90 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Ethical theories (10 marks).** An autonomous-vehicle designer must decide how the car should behave in an unavoidable crash: swerve to hit one pedestrian or continue and hit three. Analyse the situation using utilitarian, deontological, rights-based and virtue-ethics lenses (2 marks each) and then state what additional engineering and societal questions matter more than the thought experiment (2 marks).\n\n**Q2. Therac-25 (14 marks).** (a) Summarise the causes of the accidents [5]. (b) Identify at least three canons of the IEEE or NSPE codes that were violated or at risk, and explain how [5]. (c) State four engineering practices that would have prevented or limited the harm [4].\n\n**Q3. The falsified supplier data (14 marks).** You are an engineer reviewing the qualification data for a supplier's capacitors for a medical device. You notice that the lot test report shows the same measurement values (to six digits) for three different lots, suggesting copied data. Your manager says the production deadline is tomorrow and the data are \"probably fine\". (a) Apply the seven-step decision method, listing the facts, stakeholders, issues, options and your decision [10]. (b) Write a short memo (80 to 120 words) to your manager documenting your concern and the action you propose [4].\n\n**Q4. Conflicts of interest (12 marks).** For each situation, say whether it is a conflict of interest (actual, potential, or perceived), and what you should do (3 marks each): (a) You are asked to choose a vendor and your brother-in-law owns one of the bidders. (b) A supplier offers you a gift of a US$500 smartwatch after a contract award. (c) You work for a consulting firm and are assigned a project for a competitor of your former employer; you possess your former employer's confidential designs. (d) You hold a small number of shares in a company whose product you are asked to evaluate.\n\n**Q5. Whistleblowing (10 marks).** Use De George's conditions (serious harm; reported to superiors; exhausted internal channels; documented evidence; good chance of success) to evaluate the actions of engineers who warned about the Challenger O-ring risk. Where are the conditions met, and what might the engineers have done differently or additionally?\n\n**Q6. Open-source licences (8 marks).** Your team wants to use a library under the GPL v3 inside a closed-source commercial product that is distributed to customers. (a) Explain the problem [4]. (b) Give three options [4].\n\n**Q7. Privacy by design (10 marks).** A start-up designs a smart doorbell camera that records video and audio and uploads it to the cloud. (a) List five ethical or legal issues [5]. (b) Propose design measures reflecting data minimisation, security, transparency and user control [5].\n\n**Q8. Algorithmic fairness (6 marks).** A company's automated CV-screening tool selects 60 of 200 applicants from group A and 30 of 150 applicants from group B. (a) Compute the selection rates and the disparate-impact ratio and apply the four-fifths rule [3]. (b) Suggest three actions to investigate and mitigate [3].\n\n**Q9. Cost-benefit reasoning (6 marks).** A company can recall 1,000,000 units for a $12 fix per unit, or leave them and accept an estimated 50 deaths over the product's life. Using a value of a statistical life of $10 million, compute the monetary comparison and then explain why a purely monetary argument is ethically inadequate.\n\n## Model Answers and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.** **Utilitarian:** minimise total harm, so hit one rather than three (if the numbers and certainty are truly equal). **Deontological:** a duty not to use a person merely as a means, and a distinction between killing and letting die; some argue that programming a car to select a victim is impermissible, others that the duty is to follow the rule that was transparently agreed in advance. **Rights-based:** every person has an equal right to life, which cannot be traded by number; pedestrians who follow the rules have a stronger claim than those who do not. **Virtue ethics:** ask what a prudent, just and responsible designer would do: prioritise avoiding the situation altogether, be honest about limitations, and take responsibility. **More important questions:** how the system prevents the situation (speed limits, sensing and prediction, braking performance, safe testing); who is accountable; transparency of the decision rules; legal frameworks; validation evidence; and public acceptance. Real crashes are rarely clean choices; probabilities, uncertainty and the design process matter far more. (2 marks per lens with a justified conclusion; 2 for the wider questions.)\n\n**Q2.** (a) Causes: software race condition (rapid operator edits could leave the beam in the high-energy mode without the beam-flattening target in place); removal of the hardware interlocks that the Therac-20 had, so that software alone was relied upon; reuse of old software without proper re-verification; poor software engineering (no independent review, inadequate testing, no formal hazard analysis); cryptic error messages and operators trained to ignore them; the manufacturer's slow, dismissive response to reports and an over-confident risk assessment. (b) Violations: hold paramount the safety, health and welfare of the public (releasing a device with inadequate safety assurance); be honest and realistic in stating claims or estimates (claims that the system could not overdose, based on flawed analysis); perform services only in areas of competence and undertake tasks only if qualified (inadequate software competence); disclose promptly factors that might endanger the public (slow disclosure and investigation of incidents). (c) Practices: keep independent hardware interlocks (defence in depth); independent code review and rigorous testing including concurrency tests; formal hazard analysis (FMEA, fault tree) of the whole system; an incident-reporting process, with prompt investigation and notification of regulators and users; meaningful, understandable error messages and operator training; no reuse of software in a new context without re-verification. (Marks: 5 + 5 + 4.)\n\n**Q3. Model response.** (a)\n1. **Facts:** three lots show identical six-digit measurements; this is statistically implausible in independent measurements; the device is a medical device, so failures may harm patients; the manager is under deadline pressure; I do not yet know whether this is a copy-paste error in the report or fabricated data.\n2. **Stakeholders:** patients and clinicians (safety), the supplier, my company and colleagues, the manager, regulators, shareholders, me.\n3. **Issues:** public safety and health (paramount), honesty and data integrity, regulatory obligations (medical device quality management requires supplier controls and traceability), professional competence, loyalty and schedule pressure.\n4. **Options:** (i) ship as planned; (ii) say nothing; (iii) hold the affected lots, ask the supplier to explain and re-supply raw data, and run our own incoming inspection tests; (iv) escalate to quality assurance and the regulatory affairs team; (v) refuse to sign off.\n5. **Evaluation:** (i) and (ii) fail the safety, honesty and publicity tests (I would not be comfortable if a patient were harmed and my silence were known); utilitarian reasoning favours a short delay compared with potential harm; duty ethics requires honesty and care; the rights of patients demand safe devices; a person of integrity would verify first. (iii) and (iv) are proportionate: they address the risk and give the supplier a chance to explain.\n6. **Decision:** place the three lots on quality hold, inform the manager and quality assurance in writing, request the raw data and test the lots independently; escalate if the data are not satisfactorily explained or the manager insists on shipping.\n7. **Review:** document outcomes, improve supplier audits, include data-integrity checks in incoming inspection.\n(10 marks: roughly 1.5 per step, with credit for explicit use of codes and several lenses.)\n(b) **Model memo:** \"To: R. Owusu. Subject: Supplier data concern, capacitor lots 114 to 116. The test reports for lots 114, 115 and 116 contain identical measurements to six digits, which is not plausible for independent lots. Because the capacitors go into a medical device, I recommend placing these lots on hold today, asking the supplier for raw data, and running our own incoming tests (about two days). I will inform Quality Assurance. If the data are confirmed, we must also review earlier lots. Please confirm the hold by 4 p.m.\" (Marks: factual, specific, non-accusatory, proposed action, request, written record.)\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) **Actual conflict of interest** (a family tie with a bidder). Disclose it in writing to your manager, recuse yourself from the selection and the evaluation, and let others decide. (b) A **perceived/potential conflict**; the value is significant and follows a contract award. Decline politely, or report it and return it as company policy requires; many codes forbid gifts that could influence decisions. (c) A **confidentiality and legal issue** (and potential conflict). You must not use or disclose your former employer's confidential information. Tell your current employer about the restriction, avoid the tainted work, and consult legal counsel. (d) A **potential conflict** (a small financial stake). Disclose it; whether you should recuse depends on the size of the holding and the policy (a small index-fund holding may be acceptable; a significant direct holding is not). (Marks per part: 1 classification, 2 action.)\n\n**Q5.** Conditions: **serious harm**: met (loss of the shuttle and crew was foreseeable, and engineers said so). **Reported to superiors**: met (Boisjoly and others wrote memos, for example in July 1985, and argued on the eve of the launch). **Exhausted internal channels**: partly met: they argued at the teleconference and up the management chain at the contractor, but there was no appeal beyond the company, and NASA managers, not engineers, made the launch decision. **Documented evidence**: partly met: there was temperature data and photographs of seal erosion, but the evidence of temperature dependence was not presented in a clear single chart, and the engineers could not \"prove\" failure. **Good chance of success**: unclear: management had already decided to launch. Additional actions that might have helped: present the data in a more persuasive format (for example a single plot of the O-ring damage versus temperature including flights with no damage), put the recommendation in writing with signatures, ask NASA safety officials or the independent safety office directly, and refuse to sign off. After the accident, Boisjoly testified to the Rogers Commission and faced professional consequences, showing the personal cost; the case also shows that organisations need protected channels. (Marks: 5 conditions at 1 mark each with justification, 3 for alternative actions, 2 for personal costs and organisational lessons.)\n\n**Q6.** (a) The GPL is a **copyleft** licence: if you distribute a work that combines or links with GPL code, the combined work must be released under the GPL, with source code available to recipients. A closed-source product would violate the licence (and expose the company to legal and reputational risk). (b) Options: (1) **replace** the library with one under a permissive licence (MIT, BSD, Apache 2.0) or write your own; (2) **comply** by releasing the product (or the affected component) under the GPL; (3) **buy a commercial licence** from the copyright holder (dual licensing); (4) **isolate** the GPL component as a separate program communicating through a defined interface at arm's length (needs legal advice; the boundary is not always clear); (5) use it only internally without distribution. (2 marks per explanation point, 1 mark per option up to 4.)\n\n**Q7.** (a) Issues: **privacy of visitors and neighbours** who did not consent to be recorded; **consent and notice** (signs, local laws on audio recording); **data security** (cloud breaches, weak passwords, default credentials); **data retention** and secondary use (sale to third parties, sharing with police); **access control and abuse** (stalking); **bias** in any face-recognition feature; **GDPR/data protection compliance** (lawful basis, purpose limitation, right to erasure); **safety** of the device (firmware updates). (b) Measures: **minimise data**: record only on motion events, local processing where possible, limit audio, blur public areas; **secure by default**: unique credentials, end-to-end encryption in transit and at rest, signed firmware, automatic updates; **transparency**: plain-language privacy notice, a visible indicator when recording; **control**: easy deletion, export, off switch, configurable retention (for example 30 days); **accountability**: audit logs, a clear process for law-enforcement requests, a vulnerability disclosure policy. (1 mark per issue or measure.)\n\n**Q8.** (a) Step 1: Group A: 60/200 = **30 percent**.\nStep 2: Group B: 30/150 = **20 percent**.\nStep 3: Ratio = 20/30 = **0.67**, below 0.8, so under the four-fifths rule the tool shows evidence of **adverse impact** on group B (it flags the need for investigation, and is not proof of discrimination)\n(b) Actions: audit the training data and features for proxies and historical bias; test performance and error rates per group; have a human review of rejected borderline cases; validate that the criteria are job-relevant; monitor results continuously; document the model and provide an appeal channel (1 mark per correct selection rate and ratio and rule, 1 mark each for three actions.)\n\n**Q9.** Step 1: Monetary comparison: recall cost = 1,000,000 × $12 = **$12 million**.\nStep 2: Expected harm cost = 50 × $10 million = **$500 million**.\nStep 3: A purely monetary analysis favours the recall by a wide margin; but even if the numbers were reversed, the decision would remain ethically problematic.\nStep 4: Reasons: the **rights** of the affected people (informed consent, the right not to be exposed to avoidable lethal risk); the principle of **public safety paramount** in the codes (cost-benefit is not a licence to kill); the unequal distribution of costs and benefits; the uncertainty in the estimates (and the incentive to underestimate risk); the legal liability, reputation and loss of trust; and the **publicity test** (the decision would be unacceptable if made public).\nStep 5: Cost-benefit analysis is a useful input, but it cannot replace duties, rights and virtue (2 marks calculation, 4 marks for the discussion.)\n","exam":"# Engineering Communication and Ethics: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Closed book (a copy of the IEEE and NSPE codes is provided). Total: 100 marks.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** You must cite a source when you\nA) use someone else's data, figures or ideas  B) state a universally known fact  C) use your own measurements  D) write the title\n\n**2.** In most technical styles, a figure caption is placed\nA) above the figure  B) below the figure  C) on a separate page only  D) in the abstract\n\n**3.** The best subject line for an email reporting a test failure is\nA) \"Hi\"  B) \"Problem\"  C) \"Thermal test failed at 70 °C: decision needed on redesign\"  D) \"Please read!!!\"\n\n**4.** An executive summary is written mainly for\nA) decision makers who may read nothing else  B) laboratory technicians  C) the proofreader  D) the standards committee\n\n**5.** The symbol mA stands for\nA) megaamperes  B) milliamperes  C) microamperes  D) metres per ampere\n\n**6.** When you have a conflict of interest you should\nA) hide it  B) disclose it and, where necessary, recuse yourself  C) resign immediately  D) ask a friend to decide in secret\n\n**7.** The Ariane 5 Flight 501 failure was caused by\nA) a fuel leak  B) an unhandled overflow in reused inertial reference software  C) a launch pad fire  D) a faulty parachute\n\n**8.** The Hyatt Regency walkway collapse was linked to\nA) a change in how the walkway hanger rods were connected, which increased the load on the box-beam connection  B) a hurricane  C) an earthquake  D) a lightning strike\n\n**9.** Which is a basic functional-safety standard for electrical, electronic and programmable electronic systems?\nA) ISO 9001  B) IEC 61508  C) IEEE 754  D) RFC 791\n\n**10.** A CE mark on a product indicates\nA) it is made in Europe  B) the manufacturer declares conformity with applicable EU requirements  C) it is the cheapest  D) it was tested by NASA\n\n**11.** In the hierarchy of controls, the least effective measure is\nA) elimination  B) substitution  C) engineering controls  D) PPE and warnings\n\n**12.** Deontological ethics emphasises\nA) outcomes only  B) duties and rules, and respect for persons  C) profit  D) personal taste\n\n**13.** Confidentiality may be overridden when\nA) the employer is embarrassed  B) there is a serious danger to the public that cannot otherwise be prevented  C) a colleague asks  D) a competitor offers money\n\n**14.** A patent protects\nA) an idea expressed as text  B) an invention that is novel, non-obvious and useful  C) a brand name  D) a company slogan\n\n**15.** Which GDPR principle means collecting only the data you need?\nA) data minimisation  B) data maximisation  C) retention for ever  D) secret processing\n\n**16.** Under the four-fifths rule, adverse impact is indicated when a group's selection rate is below what fraction of the highest group's rate?\nA) 50 percent  B) 60 percent  C) 80 percent  D) 95 percent\n\n**17.** Under IEC 61508 (low-demand), SIL 2 corresponds to a PFDavg in the range\nA) 10^-1 to 1  B) 10^-2 to 10^-1  C) 10^-3 to 10^-2  D) 10^-4 to 10^-3\n\n**18.** When you raise a safety concern, you should\nA) rely on a phone call only  B) keep written records and copies of evidence  C) delete the emails  D) tell only friends\n\n**19.** Passive voice is acceptable in technical writing when\nA) the actor is unknown or unimportant  B) you want to sound formal  C) you want longer sentences  D) never\n\n**20.** A key technical weakness identified in the 737 MAX accidents was that MCAS\nA) used two redundant sensors  B) relied on data from a single angle-of-attack sensor  C) had no software  D) was fully documented in pilot training\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (5)** Write a subject line and a three-sentence opening paragraph for an email to your supervisor reporting that a 24-hour burn-in test of 10 units found 2 failures caused by a solder defect.\n\n**B2. (5)** For each need, choose the best format (table, line graph, block diagram or bar chart) and justify briefly: (a) the exact component values in a bill of materials; (b) how battery voltage changes over 10 hours; (c) how the sub-systems of a robot are connected; (d) the power efficiency of three designs at a single load.\n\n**B3. (5)** A student copies a 12-line function from an online forum into a coursework program and hands it in with no mention of the source. Explain whether this is acceptable, what the issues are (academic and licensing), and what the student should have done.\n\n**B4. (5)** Briefly explain what ISO 26262 is, what ASIL means (list the levels) and what changes in the development process as the ASIL level rises.\n\n**B5. (5)** (a) Three components, each with reliability 0.98, are in series. Find the system reliability [2]. (b) Two components, each with reliability 0.90, are in parallel (independent). Find the system reliability [2]. (c) State one reason why real redundancy may be worse than this calculation [1].\n\n**B6. (5)** Name the ethical theory that best matches each statement: (a) \"Choose the option that produces the greatest total benefit.\" (b) \"Some things must never be done to a person, whatever the benefits.\" (c) \"What would an honest and courageous engineer do?\" (d) \"Design the rules as if you did not know which position you would occupy in society.\" (e) \"Consider the dependence and vulnerability of the people affected.\"\n\n**B7. (5)** List the five steps an engineer should follow when considering whistleblowing, in order.\n\n**B8. (5)** Summarise the Therac-25 case in four sentences: what the system was, what went wrong technically, what went wrong organisationally, and one lesson.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 10 marks each)\n\n**C1. Abstract critique and rewrite.** A student wrote this abstract: \"This report is about a project that we did on temperature measurement. We used a sensor and a microcontroller. It worked well. Many tests were done. The results were good and the project was a success.\"\n(a) Identify at least five defects. [4]\n(b) Rewrite the abstract (120 to 160 words) using these facts: a digital temperature sensor (DS18B20) connected to a microcontroller was evaluated against a calibrated reference thermometer at 20 test points from 0 to 60 °C; the mean absolute error was 0.21 °C and the maximum error was 0.38 °C; the specified accuracy was ±0.5 °C; the response time was 4.1 s; the power consumption was 12 mW. [6]\n\n**C2. Standards and compliance.** A company plans to sell a wrist-worn device that measures heart rate. The marketing team wants to describe it as a \"general wellness\" gadget; the product team wants to add a feature that detects atrial fibrillation and alerts the user.\n(a) Explain why the intended use affects the regulatory path. [2]\n(b) List at least five standards or regulatory requirements that would apply if the device is a medical device and say what each addresses (for example IEC 60601-1, IEC 62304, ISO 14971, ISO 13485, IEC 60601-1-2 for EMC, biocompatibility ISO 10993, battery safety IEC 62133). [5]\n(c) Give a three-row hazard table (hazard, cause, severity 1 to 5, likelihood 1 to 5, risk, control) for the alerting feature. [3]\n\n**C3. Boeing 737 MAX analysis.** Using the case facts in the notes:\n(a) State the technical design decisions that increased risk. [3]\n(b) Identify the organisational and communication failures. [3]\n(c) Identify at least four canons of the IEEE or NSPE codes relevant to the engineers and managers involved and explain how. [4]\n\n**C4. Health data ethics.** A start-up has a free fitness app that collects heart rate, location and sleep data. The founders propose selling \"anonymised\" aggregate data to insurance companies.\n(a) Identify at least five ethical and legal issues. [4]\n(b) Explain why \"anonymised\" data is not automatically safe. [2]\n(c) Analyse the proposal using two ethical theories and recommend a course of action with design safeguards. [4]\n\n**C5. Integrated scenario.** A student team builds a delivery robot to be tested on a university campus. Two weeks before the demonstration, a test shows that the robot does not reliably stop for pedestrians who step in front of it within 1 m at full speed (0.8 m/s): in 3 of 20 trials it stopped too late. The faculty sponsor wants the demo to go ahead as planned.\n(a) Write three measurable safety requirements for pedestrian protection. [3]\n(b) Propose a hazard analysis (identify hazard, severity, likelihood, risk before and after controls) and apply the hierarchy of controls. [3]\n(c) Decide whether the demonstration should proceed, and justify your decision with at least two ethical lenses and the codes. [2]\n(d) Write a two-sentence message to the sponsor. [2]\n\n---\n\n# Marking Scheme and Complete Solutions\n\n## Section A answers\n1 **A** · 2 **B** · 3 **C** · 4 **A** · 5 **B** · 6 **B** · 7 **B** · 8 **A** · 9 **B** · 10 **B** · 11 **D** · 12 **B** · 13 **B** · 14 **B** · 15 **A** · 16 **C** · 17 **C** · 18 **B** · 19 **A** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons:\n1. Credit anything that is not yours.\n2. Figure captions below, table captions above.\n3. Informative, specific, and states the action needed.\n4. Decision makers.\n5. The prefix m means milli, 10^-3.\n6. Transparency first.\n7. A 64-bit floating-point value was converted to a 16-bit integer and overflowed.\n8. The change from continuous rods to offset rods doubled the load on the box beam.\n9. IEC 61508.\n10. It is a declaration by the manufacturer, not a certification of quality.\n11. Warnings and PPE depend on human behaviour.\n12. Kant and the duty-based tradition.\n13. The public interest overrides confidentiality only in serious cases.\n14. Patents protect inventions.\n15. Data minimisation.\n16. 80 percent.\n17. 10^-3 to 10^-2.\n18. Written records protect you and the public.\n19. For example \"The samples were heated\" when the actor is unimportant.\n20. Single angle-of-attack sensor.\n\n## Section B solutions\n\n**B1.** Subject: **\"Burn-in test: 2 of 10 units failed, solder defect identified, rework plan proposed.\"** Opening: \"The 24-hour burn-in test of 10 units finished this morning: 8 passed and 2 failed (units 4 and 7). Inspection shows cold solder joints on connector J3 in both failed units. I propose reworking all remaining boards and repeating the burn-in, which will add three days to the schedule; I would like your approval by Thursday.\" (Marks: 1 subject, 1 result and numbers, 1 cause, 1 action and impact, 1 professional tone.)\n\n**B2.** (a) **Table:** exact values need to be read. (b) **Line graph:** a trend over time (continuous variable). (c) **Block diagram:** shows structure and signal flow. (d) **Bar chart** (or table): compares a single quantity across a small number of discrete categories. (1 mark for format, brief justification for each, 0.25 each.)\n\n**B3.** **Not acceptable.** It is plagiarism (presenting another person's work as your own) unless attribution is given and the course permits it; it is also a **licensing** matter: forum code usually carries a licence (for example Stack Overflow content is under Creative Commons BY-SA, which requires attribution and share-alike), so copying without credit breaches the licence. The student should have **cited the source in a comment and in the report**, checked the licence and the course rules, understood and tested the code, and preferably written the solution in their own words. (2 marks for plagiarism, 2 for licensing, 1 for the correct practice.)\n\n**B4.** Step 1: ISO 26262 is the **functional safety standard for road vehicles** (electrical/electronic systems), adapted from IEC 61508.\nStep 2: **ASIL** (Automotive Safety Integrity Level) classifies the risk of a hazard by severity, exposure and controllability: **QM** (quality management, no ASIL), **A, B, C, D** (D is the most stringent).\nStep 3: As the ASIL rises, the development process requires more rigour: stronger verification methods and coverage metrics (for example MC/DC in software for ASIL D), more independent reviews and assessment, redundancy and diagnostic coverage, and fuller documentation and traceability (1 mark scope, 2 for ASIL and levels, 2 for process changes.)\n\n**B5.** (a) Step 1: series: R = 0.98³ = 0.98 × 0.98 × 0.98 = **0.941** (about 94.1 percent).\n(b) Step 2: parallel: R = 1 − (1 − 0.90)² = 1 − 0.01 = **0.99**.\n(c) **Common-cause failures** (the same defect, same power supply, same software bug, same environmental event) break the independence assumption, so the real reliability is lower. (Other valid reasons: failure of the switching or voting mechanism.)\n\n**B6.** (a) **Utilitarianism.** (b) **Deontology** (or rights-based ethics). (c) **Virtue ethics.** (d) **Justice as fairness (Rawls).** (e) **Ethics of care.** (1 mark each.)\n\n**B7.** (1) Verify the facts and your competence. (2) Raise the concern internally in writing, first with your supervisor. (3) Escalate through further internal channels (higher management, ethics office, ombudsperson). (4) If the danger is serious and internal channels fail, report to the appropriate external authority (regulator, professional body), after seeking advice. (5) Keep records and protect evidence throughout, and seek legal and professional support. (1 mark each, order matters.)\n\n**B8.** Model answer: \"Therac-25 was a computer-controlled radiation therapy machine (1985 to 1987). Software race conditions, together with the removal of the hardware interlocks used on its predecessor, allowed the beam to deliver massive overdoses, killing or injuring several patients. Organisationally, the manufacturer had no independent software review, dismissed early reports, gave operators cryptic error messages and was slow to investigate. Safety-critical systems need defence in depth and a culture that investigates every incident.\" (1 mark per sentence plus 1 for accuracy.)\n\n## Section C solutions\n\n**C1.**\n(a) Defects: no context or problem statement; \"a sensor\" and \"a microcontroller\" are unspecified; no objective; no method (what was tested and how); no quantitative results (\"worked well\", \"good\" are vague); no conclusion tied to the specification; passive \"many tests were done\" hides the actor and number; the first sentence (\"This report is about...\") wastes words; no mention of limitations. (Any five, with 0.8 marks each.)\n(b) **Model rewrite (about 135 words):** \"Low-power temperature monitoring requires sensors that meet a stated accuracy at modest cost. This study evaluated the DS18B20 digital temperature sensor, connected to a microcontroller, against a calibrated reference thermometer. Twenty test points between 0 and 60 °C were measured in a stirred bath. The mean absolute error was 0.21 °C and the maximum error was 0.38 °C, within the specified accuracy of ±0.5 °C. The response time was 4.1 s, and the circuit consumed 12 mW. These results show that the sensor and microcontroller combination meets the accuracy requirement across the tested range and is suitable for slowly changing temperatures, but its 4.1 s response time limits its use for rapid transients.\" Checklist (1 mark each, 6): background/problem; objective; method with test range and number of points; the two error values; comparison with specification; response time and power; conclusion; length and standalone.\n\n**C2.**\n(a) Regulators judge a product by its **intended use** and the claims made (including marketing and the software features). A heart-rate display for general wellness is typically low-risk and lightly regulated, whereas a feature that **detects a disease (atrial fibrillation) and alerts the user** is a diagnostic claim, so the device is a medical device (for example FDA clearance in the US, or CE marking under the EU Medical Device Regulation), with much stricter requirements, clinical evidence and post-market surveillance.\n(b) Examples: **ISO 13485** (quality management system for medical devices); **ISO 14971** (risk management for medical devices); **IEC 62304** (medical device software life cycle, with software safety classes A, B, C); **IEC 60601-1** (basic safety and essential performance of medical electrical equipment); **IEC 60601-1-2** (EMC for medical electrical equipment); **ISO 10993** series (biocompatibility of skin-contact materials); **IEC 62133** (safety of the lithium battery) and **IEC 62366** (usability engineering); plus data protection law for health data. (1 mark each for five items with their scope.)\n(c) Sample table:\n- **Hazard:** false negative (missed atrial fibrillation). **Cause:** algorithm error, poor skin contact or motion artefact. **S:** 4, **L:** 3, **Risk:** 12. **Controls:** validation on a clinical dataset, signal-quality check, labelling that the device is not a substitute for medical advice.\n- **Hazard:** false positive alert causing anxiety and unnecessary treatment. **Cause:** artefact, algorithm threshold. **S:** 3, **L:** 3, **Risk:** 9. **Controls:** confirmation step, clear wording, referral to a clinician.\n- **Hazard:** skin burn or irritation. **Cause:** battery or sensor heating, allergic reaction. **S:** 4, **L:** 1, **Risk:** 4. **Controls:** temperature limit, battery protection circuit, biocompatible materials.\n(1 mark per row with a sensible hazard, scores and control.)\n\n**C3.**\n(a) Design decisions: MCAS relied on **a single angle-of-attack sensor**, so one faulty sensor could trigger repeated nose-down commands; MCAS authority was increased during development (it could move the stabiliser by a larger amount than first planned) and could act repeatedly; the failure condition was not classified at the correct severity; there was no AOA disagree alert as a standard feature; and the design assumed that pilots would react within a short time to a runaway trim. (b) Organisational and communication failures: pressure to avoid costly retraining and to compete with a rival aircraft; limited disclosure of MCAS to pilots, airlines and, in part, to the regulator; reliance on the manufacturer to certify its own designs (delegated authority); inadequate escalation of internal concerns; a culture that prioritised schedule and cost. (c) Canons: **hold paramount the safety, health and welfare of the public** (NSPE 1, IEEE 1); **issue public statements only in an objective and truthful manner** and **be honest and realistic in stating claims** (NSPE 3, IEEE 3: statements about MCAS and training needs); **perform services only in areas of competence** and **disclose promptly factors that might endanger the public** (IEEE 1, NSPE 2); **avoid deceptive acts** (NSPE 5); **support colleagues in following the code** and **seek, accept and offer honest criticism of technical work** (IEEE 7 and 10: encourage speaking up). (Marks: 3 + 3 + 4, 1 per canon with a link to the case.)\n\n**C4.**\n(a) Issues: **informed consent** (users may not realise their data will be sold); **privacy of sensitive health and location data**; **re-identification risk**; **discrimination** (insurers could raise premiums or deny cover); **data security and breaches**; **legal compliance** (GDPR: lawful basis, purpose limitation, data minimisation, right to erasure; health-data rules); **fairness and power imbalance** (a free app, users pay with their data); **transparency**. (b) Anonymised data can often be re-identified by linking it with other datasets (for example location traces uniquely identify people); small groups in \"aggregate\" data can reveal individuals; and combining data sources enables inference. Genuine anonymisation requires techniques such as aggregation with minimum group sizes, differential privacy, and strict contractual and technical controls. (c) **Utilitarian:** possible benefits (funding the service, research insights, cheaper insurance for healthy people) versus harms (privacy loss, discrimination); the harms fall on vulnerable users and are hard to reverse, so the benefits are unlikely to outweigh them without safeguards. **Rights-based/deontological:** users have a right to control sensitive personal data and not to be used merely as a means of profit; consent must be explicit, specific and freely given. **Recommendation:** do not sell data to insurers; if monetisation is required, use opt-in consent, offer paid ad-free tiers, share only aggregated and differentially private statistics for research with ethics review, provide deletion and export, publish a clear policy and appoint a data protection officer. (Marks: 4 + 2 + 4.)\n\n**C5.**\n(a) Sample requirements: \"The robot shall come to a complete stop within 0.5 m (measured from the sensing of an obstacle) when an obstacle appears at 1.0 m at the maximum speed of 0.8 m/s, in 100 out of 100 consecutive trials.\" \"The robot shall limit its speed to 0.5 m/s whenever pedestrians are detected within 5 m.\" \"The robot shall provide a hardware emergency stop button reachable on the vehicle and a remote emergency stop with a response time under 100 ms.\" (1 mark each: measurable, with numbers, units, conditions.)\n(b) Hazard: **collision with a pedestrian at close range**. Before controls: severity 3 (injury, minor to moderate), likelihood 3 (3 out of 20 trials failed), risk 9. Controls by level: **eliminate/substitute**: demonstrate in a closed area with no pedestrians; **engineering**: reduce speed to 0.4 m/s, add a bumper contact switch cutting motor power, add a second (independent) sensor, a hardware E-stop; **administrative**: safety marshals, barriers, a trained operator holding a remote E-stop, written test procedure; **PPE**: not applicable to bystanders (high-visibility vests for staff). After controls: severity 2, likelihood 1, risk 2. (3 marks.)\n(c) **Decision:** the demonstration should not proceed in the planned form (full speed near pedestrians); it may proceed in a modified, controlled form after re-testing. **Consequentialist:** a collision could injure someone and damage the team's and university's reputation; the cost of delay or modification is small. **Rights/deontological:** pedestrians have not consented to be test subjects. **Codes:** the public's safety is paramount; engineers must disclose known risks and are accountable for what they sign off. (2 marks.)\n(d) Model message: \"Our latest test shows the robot failed to stop in time in 3 of 20 trials at full speed, so I recommend we do not demonstrate it near pedestrians until we have reduced speed and added an independent bumper stop and retested. We can still hold the demonstration in a barrier-protected area with a safety marshal and a remote emergency stop, and I will send you the test report by Friday.\" (1 mark for the clear facts and recommendation, 1 mark for alternative and tone.)\n","notes":"# Engineering Communication and Ethics: Complete Lecture Notes\n\nEngineers are paid to design, build and decide, but almost everything they do reaches other people through words, diagrams and numbers: reports, specifications, emails, code comments, presentations. Failures of communication and failures of ethics are the root cause of many engineering disasters. This course gives you a working method for each.\n\n## Part 1. Technical Communication\n\n## Chapter 1. Principles of Technical Writing\n\n### 1.1 Audience, purpose, context\nBefore writing, answer five questions:\n1. **Who** will read this (manager, client, fellow engineer, regulator, the public)? What do they already know?\n2. **Why** are they reading (to decide, to act, to learn, to verify)?\n3. **What** is the single main message (the \"bottom line up front\")?\n4. **How** will they use it (skim, search, study, act on it immediately)?\n5. **What** form is expected (memo, report, email, standard, README)?\nA good technical document lets a busy reader find the answer in 30 seconds and still supports a careful reader who checks the details.\n\n### 1.2 The writing process\nPlan (audience, purpose, outline) → draft (get ideas down, do not polish) → revise (structure, content, completeness) → edit (sentences, clarity, consistency) → proofread (spelling, units, numbers, references). Leave time between drafting and revising. Revise from the top down: structure first, sentences last.\n\n### 1.3 Style rules that matter\n- **Be concise and concrete.** Prefer \"The motor overheated at 85 °C\" to \"Thermal issues were encountered.\"\n- **Use active voice** when the actor matters: \"The controller sends a reset\" rather than \"A reset is sent.\" Passive voice is acceptable when the actor is unimportant or unknown.\n- **One idea per sentence, one topic per paragraph.** Start paragraphs with a topic sentence. Aim for sentences of 15 to 25 words on average.\n- **Use precise terms and keep them consistent.** Do not alternate between \"voltage\", \"potential\" and \"level\" for the same quantity. Define abbreviations and symbols at first use.\n- **Avoid vague quantifiers** (\"very\", \"several\", \"fast\"). Give numbers with units and tolerances: \"response time < 50 ms (95th percentile)\".\n- **Avoid noun stacks and jargon** that the reader may not share. \"Data link layer retransmission timeout parameter\" becomes \"the timeout before the data link layer retransmits.\"\n- **Parallel structure** in lists and headings. **Concrete verbs** instead of \"make\", \"do\", \"perform\".\n- **Cut redundancy**: \"in order to\" → \"to\"; \"due to the fact that\" → \"because\"; \"at this point in time\" → \"now\".\n\n### 1.4 Numbers, units and symbols\n- Use SI units with a space between number and unit (5.0 V, 20 kHz); symbols for quantities in italics, units upright.\n- Report significant figures consistent with the precision of the data; state uncertainty (3.30 ± 0.05 V).\n- Be careful with orders of magnitude and prefixes: mA versus MA, mb versus Mb, MB versus Mb (megabyte versus megabit), kΩ.\n- Define every symbol in an equation. Number important equations and refer to them in the text.\n\n### 1.5 Visuals: figures, tables, equations\n- Each figure or table must be **referred to in the text**, numbered, and have a caption (figure captions below the figure, table captions above the table in most styles).\n- A graph needs labelled axes with units, readable fonts, distinguishable markers (also in greyscale), a legend if more than one series, and error bars where relevant. Do not use 3D effects or truncated axes that mislead.\n- Use a table for exact values, a graph for trends and comparisons, a diagram (block diagram, schematic, flowchart, UML) for structure.\n- Block diagram: label every block and signal, show direction of flow, group by function.\n- Make visuals self-explanatory enough that a reader can understand them with the caption alone.\n\n## Chapter 2. Engineering Documents\n\n### 2.1 The IMRaD structure for reports and papers\n- **Title** (specific and informative), **Abstract** (150 to 250 words: background, objective, method, main results with numbers, conclusion), **Introduction** (problem, context, objective), **Method** (enough detail to reproduce), **Results** (what you found, with figures and tables), **Discussion** (what it means, limitations, comparison with expectations or literature), **Conclusion** (answers the objective; future work), **References**, **Appendices**.\n- An **executive summary** differs from an abstract: it is written for decision makers, stresses findings, costs, risks and recommendations, and stands alone on one page.\n\n### 2.2 Laboratory and design reports\nA lab report states the objective, theory (briefly), apparatus, procedure, raw data, analysis (sample calculations, uncertainty, graphs), discussion (compare with theory, list error sources and their effect) and conclusions. A **design report** adds requirements, constraints, alternatives considered with a decision matrix, the chosen design with justification, test results against requirements, and risks.\n\n### 2.3 Proposals, specifications and requirements\n- A **proposal** persuades: problem, proposed solution, benefits, plan and schedule, budget, qualifications, risks.\n- A **requirements specification** states what the system shall do. Good requirements are **necessary, unambiguous, measurable, feasible, testable and traceable**. Use \"shall\" for mandatory statements: \"The system shall respond to a valid request within 200 ms.\" Avoid \"fast\", \"user-friendly\", \"etc.\" Distinguish functional requirements (what) from non-functional (performance, safety, usability, reliability).\n- A **test plan** links each requirement to a test with pass/fail criteria.\n\n### 2.4 Memos, emails and meeting minutes\n- **Memo or email:** a clear subject line, the main point in the first two sentences, then supporting details, action items with owners and dates, and a polite close. One topic per email.\n- **Minutes:** date, attendees, decisions, actions (who, what, by when).\n- Tone: professional, direct, respectful; never write anything you would not want read aloud in front of a client.\n\n### 2.5 Software and technical documentation\n- A **README** explains what the project is, how to install and run it, an example, how to contribute and the license.\n- **API documentation** states each function's purpose, parameters, return values, errors and an example. Comments explain **why**, not what.\n- Use version control for documents too; docs-as-code (Markdown, LaTeX) keeps text and history together.\n- A good **user manual** is task-oriented (steps, screenshots, warnings), tested on a real beginner.\n\n### 2.6 Résumés and cover letters\nOne page for early career: contact details, education, projects (with outcomes and numbers), skills, experience. Tailor to the role. Use strong verbs (\"designed\", \"reduced\", \"implemented\") and quantify results (\"cut latency by 35 percent\").\n\n## Chapter 3. Citing Sources, Plagiarism and Integrity in Writing\n\n### 3.1 Why and how to cite\nCite to give credit, let readers verify, and show the evidence behind your claims. Cite when you use another person's words, ideas, data, figures, code or designs.\n**IEEE style** (common in engineering): numbered references in square brackets in order of first appearance, e.g. \"as shown in [2]\". Reference list entries:\n- Journal: [1] A. Author, B. Author, \"Title of article,\" *Journal Abbrev.*, vol. 12, no. 3, pp. 100-110, Mar. 2021.\n- Book: [2] A. Author, *Title of Book*, 3rd ed. City, Country: Publisher, 2019.\n- Standard: [3] *IEEE Standard for Ethernet*, IEEE Std 802.3-2022, 2022.\n- Web page: [4] Organization, \"Title of page,\" Site name. URL (accessed Mon. DD, YYYY).\nOther styles (APA, Harvard) use author-date citations. Be consistent within a document.\n\n### 3.2 Quoting, paraphrasing, summarising\n- **Direct quotation:** in quotation marks with a citation; use sparingly.\n- **Paraphrase:** restate in your own words and structure, with citation. Changing a few words is still plagiarism.\n- **Summary:** a condensed account of the main points, with citation.\n- Common knowledge need not be cited, but when in doubt cite.\n\n### 3.3 Plagiarism and academic integrity\nPlagiarism is presenting someone else's work as your own. It includes copying text, code or figures without credit, self-plagiarism (reusing your earlier submitted work without permission), fabricating or falsifying data, and unauthorised collaboration. In engineering, falsified data can kill people, so integrity is a safety issue as well as an academic one. Using AI tools: follow the course or employer policy, disclose use where required, verify every claim, and remember you are accountable for the result.\n\n### 3.4 Copyright, licences and attribution\nCopyright arises automatically for original works. Open-source licences: **permissive** (MIT, BSD, Apache 2.0: use freely with attribution) versus **copyleft** (GPL: derived works must be released under the same licence). Creative Commons licences describe reuse of text and images. Check the licence before you reuse code, data or images, and keep attributions.\n\n## Chapter 4. Oral and Visual Communication\n\n### 4.1 Presentations\n- Structure: tell them what the problem is, why it matters, what you did, what you found, what to do next. One message per slide, headline sentences as slide titles.\n- **Slides:** minimal text (no paragraphs), large fonts (at least 24 pt), clear figures, consistent layout, and a final summary slide. Rehearse with a timer; allow about one to two minutes per slide.\n- **Delivery:** face the audience, speak slowly, avoid reading the slide, explain each figure (axes first, then the message). Prepare for questions; it is acceptable to say \"I do not know, I will check and follow up.\"\n- Poster: a title, three to five main panels, large figures, readable from 1.5 m.\n\n### 4.2 Meetings and teamwork\nSet an agenda and goals, assign roles (chair, scribe), record decisions and actions, share the notes promptly. In teams, agree on responsibilities in writing, use version control and an issue tracker, give feedback on work (not on the person), and resolve conflicts early using interests rather than positions. Tuckman's stages: forming, storming, norming, performing, adjourning.\n\n### 4.3 Cross-cultural and accessible communication\nUse plain English for non-native readers; avoid idioms and ambiguous abbreviations. Give dates in an unambiguous format (2026-03-14), specify time zones. Accessible documents have alt text for images, sufficient colour contrast, no reliance on colour alone, and headings for screen readers.\n\n## Part 2. Standards\n\n## Chapter 5. Engineering Standards\n\n### 5.1 What a standard is and why it matters\nA **standard** is a document, agreed by consensus and approved by a recognised body, that provides rules, guidelines or characteristics for activities or their results, to achieve an optimum degree of order. Benefits: **interoperability** (USB, Wi-Fi, Ethernet), **safety**, **quality**, **economies of scale**, **market access**, and **legal defence** (compliance shows due care). A **code** (for example a building or electrical code) is a standard adopted into law. A **regulation** is legally binding. De facto standards arise from market dominance; **de jure** standards are formally approved.\n\n### 5.2 Who writes standards\n- **ISO** (International Organization for Standardization) and **IEC** (International Electrotechnical Commission): global; **ITU** for telecommunications.\n- **IEEE** and its Standards Association: electrical, electronic, computing and communications standards (for example IEEE 802.3 Ethernet, IEEE 802.11 Wi-Fi, IEEE 754 floating point).\n- **IETF** publishes Internet standards as RFCs (RFC 791 IPv4, RFC 9293 TCP).\n- **ANSI** coordinates the US system; **BSI** (UK), **DIN** (Germany), **JIS** (Japan), **SANS/SABS** (South Africa), **GSA** (Ghana) and similar national bodies; **CEN/CENELEC** in Europe; **ASTM** for materials; **UL** for product safety; **NFPA** (NFPA 70 is the US National Electrical Code).\n\n### 5.3 Examples you should know\n- **ISO 9001**: quality management systems. **ISO/IEC 27001**: information security management. **ISO 14001**: environmental management.\n- **IEC 61508**: functional safety of electrical, electronic and programmable electronic systems (the parent of sector standards). **ISO 26262**: functional safety of road vehicles (ASIL levels A to D). **DO-178C**: software in airborne systems. **IEC 62304**: medical device software lifecycle. **IEC 60601**: safety of medical electrical equipment.\n- **IEC 61000**: electromagnetic compatibility. **FCC Part 15** (US) and **CE marking** (EU): conformity requirements for electronic products. **RoHS** limits hazardous substances in electronics. **WEEE**: electronic waste.\n- **IEEE 754**: floating-point arithmetic; **POSIX**: operating system interfaces; **Unicode**: text.\n- **ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148**: requirements engineering; **ISO/IEC 25010**: software quality model.\n\n### 5.4 Reading and applying a standard\n1. Identify the scope and applicability (does it cover your product and market?).\n2. Distinguish **normative** clauses (mandatory, \"shall\") from **informative** ones (\"should\", \"may\", notes, annexes).\n3. Note the edition and date; standards are revised. Cite the exact edition.\n4. Trace each requirement to a design decision and a test; keep a compliance matrix with evidence.\n5. If you deviate, document the justification and the risk assessment. Do not claim compliance you have not verified.\nStandards cost money and are copyrighted; use library or institutional access, and do not share copies illegally.\n\n### 5.5 Standards, safety and risk\n**Risk = probability (likelihood) × severity (consequence).** A risk matrix classifies hazards as low, medium or high. The standard approach (ALARP: as low as reasonably practicable) uses a hierarchy of controls: (1) eliminate the hazard, (2) substitute, (3) engineering controls (interlocks, redundancy, fail-safe design), (4) administrative controls (procedures, training, warnings), (5) personal protective equipment. Warnings and manuals are the weakest control. **Safety integrity levels** (SIL in IEC 61508, ASIL in ISO 26262) set how rigorous development and verification must be as risk rises. **Hazard analysis methods**: FMEA (failure modes and effects analysis), fault tree analysis, HAZOP, STPA.\n\n## Part 3. Professional Ethics\n\n## Chapter 6. Foundations of Engineering Ethics\n\n### 6.1 Ethics, law, and professionalism\n**Ethics** is the study of what we ought to do; **law** is what we must do; **professionalism** includes competence, honesty, accountability, and service to society. Something can be legal and unethical (selling a product known to be hazardous in a market with weak regulation) or illegal yet defensible (rare, and requires serious justification). Engineers hold a special position: the public relies on their expertise and cannot check it, so the **public's safety, health and welfare come first**.\n\n### 6.2 Ethical theories\n- **Utilitarianism (consequentialism):** choose the action that produces the greatest good for the greatest number. Tools: cost-benefit analysis, risk-benefit analysis. Weakness: can justify harming a minority; hard to measure and value outcomes.\n- **Deontology (duty ethics, Kant):** some duties and rules must be respected regardless of outcomes (do not lie, respect persons as ends and not merely means). Weakness: rules can conflict.\n- **Rights theories:** people have rights (safety, privacy, informed consent) that limit what can be done to them.\n- **Virtue ethics (Aristotle):** ask what a person of good character (honest, courageous, fair, prudent) would do.\n- **Justice and fairness (Rawls):** consider the least advantaged; ask what rules you would accept without knowing your own position.\n- **Ethics of care:** attend to relationships and vulnerability.\nUse several lenses; where they agree, the answer is strong; where they disagree, reason carefully.\n\n### 6.3 Professional codes of ethics\n- **IEEE Code of Ethics:** members commit to hold paramount the safety, health and welfare of the public; to avoid real or perceived conflicts of interest; to be honest and realistic in stating claims and estimates; to reject bribery; to improve the understanding of technology and its consequences; to maintain and improve technical competence and undertake tasks only if qualified; to seek and give honest criticism and credit others' contributions; to treat all persons fairly and without discrimination; to avoid injuring others, their property, reputation or employment; and to support colleagues in following the code.\n- **NSPE Code:** six **Fundamental Canons**: (1) hold paramount the safety, health and welfare of the public; (2) perform services only in areas of competence; (3) issue public statements only in an objective and truthful manner; (4) act for each employer or client as faithful agents or trustees; (5) avoid deceptive acts; (6) conduct themselves honourably, responsibly, ethically and lawfully so as to enhance the honour, reputation and usefulness of the profession.\n- **ACM Code of Ethics (2018):** contribute to society and human well-being; avoid harm; be honest and trustworthy; be fair and take action not to discriminate; respect the work required to produce new ideas, inventions and creative works; respect privacy; honour confidentiality; plus professional responsibilities (high quality, competence, laws, review) and leadership principles.\nCodes are not algorithms: they give priorities and reasons, and engineers must still use judgement. Where codes conflict with an employer's instructions, the public interest takes precedence.\n\n### 6.4 A method for ethical decision making\n1. **State the problem** and the facts (what is known, unknown, and who is affected).\n2. **Identify stakeholders** and their interests and rights.\n3. **List the ethical issues** and relevant duties, codes, laws and standards.\n4. **Generate options**, including creative middle ways.\n5. **Evaluate each option** with several lenses (consequences, duties, rights, character, fairness) and the \"tests\": the **publicity test** (would I be comfortable if this were on the front page?), the **role-model test**, the **reversibility test** (would I accept this if I were the affected party?).\n6. **Decide** and plan the action, including who must be told and how to document it.\n7. **Review** the outcome and learn from it.\nDocument facts and decisions in writing at the time.\n\n## Chapter 7. Responsibilities and Dilemmas\n\n### 7.1 Safety and risk\nEngineers have a duty to design for safety, to estimate risks honestly, and to **inform clients and the public** of dangers. Risk acceptability depends on voluntariness, control, immediacy, and severity of consequences; the public tends to accept voluntary risks (driving) more readily than imposed ones (a nearby chemical plant). **Informed consent** requires that affected people receive the relevant information in understandable form. A **precautionary approach** favours caution when severe or irreversible harm is possible and evidence is incomplete.\n\n### 7.2 Honesty, data and conflicts of interest\n- Do not fabricate, falsify or selectively report data. Report negative results. Honest error is correctable; deception is not.\n- **Conflict of interest:** a situation in which professional judgement might be compromised by a personal interest (gifts, financial stakes, family ties). Disclose it and, when necessary, step aside. Bribery and kickbacks are forbidden; modest gifts depend on the policy and context.\n- **Confidentiality** protects employers' and clients' proprietary information, but it does not override the duty to protect the public from serious danger.\n\n### 7.3 Whistleblowing and dissent\nWhen a serious danger to the public is concealed or ignored, an engineer may have to report it. Steps: (1) check the facts and your own competence; (2) raise the concern internally and in writing with your supervisor, then up the chain; (3) use ethics hotlines or ombudspersons; (4) if the danger is serious and imminent and internal channels fail, report to the appropriate regulator or professional body; (5) keep careful records and seek advice (legal counsel, professional society). Many jurisdictions protect whistleblowers, but retaliation still occurs, so plan carefully. De George's conditions list seriousness of harm, reporting internally first, exhausting internal channels, strong evidence, and reasonable chance of success.\n\n### 7.4 Intellectual property\n**Patents** protect inventions (novel, non-obvious, useful) for about 20 years in exchange for disclosure. **Copyright** protects expression (code, text, images), **trademarks** protect brands, **trade secrets** protect confidential business information. Employment contracts often assign inventions to the employer. Respect others' IP, check licences, and avoid disclosing secrets.\n\n### 7.5 Privacy, security and data ethics\nCollect only the data you need, protect it (encryption, access control), and be transparent about use. Laws such as the EU GDPR (consent, purpose limitation, data minimisation, right to erasure) and national data protection acts (for example Ghana's Data Protection Act, 2012) create duties. Security is an ethical duty: ship secure defaults, patch vulnerabilities, use **responsible disclosure** when you find a flaw in someone else's system.\n\n### 7.6 Sustainability, society and emerging technologies\nConsider the full life cycle: materials, energy use, e-waste, repairability, end-of-life recycling. Consider impacts on disadvantaged communities and the environment (the UN Sustainable Development Goals provide a framework). For AI and automated systems: **bias** in data and models, **transparency and explainability**, **accountability** (a human or an organisation must be answerable), **safety** and **human oversight**, and the right of people to contest automated decisions.\n\n### 7.7 Professional licensing and competence\nMany jurisdictions license engineers (Professional Engineer in the US, Chartered Engineer in the UK and Commonwealth, registered engineer with national bodies such as the Ghana Institution of Engineering). Licensure requires education, supervised experience, examination and adherence to a code of conduct. Practise only within your competence; continued professional development (CPD) is part of the duty.\n\n## Chapter 8. Case Studies (what happened, what went wrong, lessons)\n\n### 8.1 Therac-25 (1985 to 1987)\nA computer-controlled radiation therapy machine produced by Atomic Energy of Canada Limited. At least six patients received massive radiation overdoses and several died. Causes: **software race conditions** (a fast operator editing the treatment settings could leave the machine in an unsafe state), **removal of the hardware interlocks** used on the earlier Therac-20 and reliance on software alone, reused software that was assumed safe, no independent code review, inadequate testing, cryptic error messages (\"Malfunction 54\") that operators learned to dismiss, and a slow response to early reports. Lessons: **defence in depth**; do not trust software alone for safety; test integrated systems; investigate every incident; a good safety culture listens to users.\n\n### 8.2 Space Shuttle Challenger (28 January 1986)\nThe shuttle broke apart 73 seconds after launch when a joint seal (O-ring) in a solid rocket booster failed in unusually cold weather (about 2 °C at launch). Engineers at the contractor Morton Thiokol, including Roger Boisjoly, had warned about cold-temperature seal erosion and recommended against launching; management reversed the recommendation under schedule pressure. Seven crew members died. Lessons: **safety concerns must be heard and documented**; data should be presented clearly (the evidence of temperature dependence was in the flight records but not displayed convincingly); \"normalisation of deviance\" (accepting repeated anomalies as normal); the conflict between engineering and management judgement; whistleblowing and its personal costs.\n\n### 8.3 Hyatt Regency walkway collapse (17 July 1981)\nTwo suspended walkways collapsed in a Kansas City hotel lobby, killing 114 people. A design change during construction (hanging the lower walkway from the upper one by separate rods instead of one continuous rod) doubled the load on the upper box-beam connections, which were barely adequate even in the original design. The change was approved by phone without a proper calculation check. The engineers lost their licences. Lessons: **review and verify design changes**; clear responsibility for approval; communication between designer and fabricator; do not assume that someone else checked.\n\n### 8.4 Ariane 5 Flight 501 (4 June 1996)\nThe rocket veered off course and self-destructed about 37 seconds after launch (loss of about US$370 million of equipment). The inertial reference software, reused from Ariane 4, converted a 64-bit floating-point value of horizontal velocity to a 16-bit signed integer; the larger Ariane 5 trajectory produced a value that overflowed. The exception was not handled and both redundant units (running the same code) failed. Lessons: reuse requires re-verification in the new context; **redundancy of identical software does not protect against a design error**; test with realistic inputs; handle exceptions deliberately.\n\n### 8.5 Boeing 737 MAX (2018 and 2019)\nTwo accidents (Lion Air Flight 610 in October 2018 with 189 deaths and Ethiopian Airlines Flight 302 in March 2019 with 157 deaths) were linked to the **MCAS** flight control software, which relied on a single angle-of-attack sensor and could repeatedly push the nose down. Investigations found problems with design assumptions about pilot response, the decision to depend on one sensor, limited disclosure to pilots and regulators of how MCAS worked, and organisational pressure to avoid costly pilot retraining. The aircraft was grounded worldwide for about 20 months. Lessons: **safety-critical functions need redundancy and honest hazard analysis**; transparency with regulators and users; schedule and commercial pressure must not override safety; the role of independent oversight.\n\n### 8.6 Volkswagen emissions scandal (2015)\nVolkswagen installed software \"defeat devices\" in about 11 million diesel vehicles worldwide that detected emissions tests and reduced pollutant output only during testing, so that on the road the cars emitted far more nitrogen oxides than allowed. The US EPA issued a notice of violation in September 2015. Executives and engineers faced criminal charges and the company paid very large penalties. Lessons: **engineers cannot hide behind \"following orders\"**; deliberate deception violates every professional code; the environmental and health costs fall on the public; speak up early.\n\n### 8.7 Uber self-driving vehicle fatality (18 March 2018)\nA test vehicle operating in automated mode in Tempe, Arizona struck and killed a pedestrian, Elaine Herzberg. The US National Transportation Safety Board found that the system failed to classify and predict the pedestrian's path correctly, that automatic emergency braking was disabled in autonomous mode, that the human safety driver was distracted, and that the company's safety culture and oversight were inadequate. Lessons: **testing on public roads imposes risk on non-consenting people**; human oversight must be realistic (automation complacency); safety cases and organisational safety culture matter.\n\n## Chapter 9. Common Mistakes Checklist\n1. Writing for yourself instead of the reader; burying the main point.\n2. Vague, unmeasurable statements and requirements.\n3. Missing units, undefined symbols, unlabelled axes, figures not referred to in the text.\n4. Close paraphrase without citation; citing a source but not marking quoted text.\n5. Treating a code of ethics as a rulebook to be matched mechanically rather than a guide to reasoning.\n6. Assuming that legality equals ethics, or that \"everyone else does it\" is an excuse.\n7. Reporting a problem verbally only, with no written record.\n8. Claiming compliance with a standard without evidence, or citing an outdated edition.\n","plan":"# Engineering Communication and Ethics: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 6 to 8 hours per week (2 reading, 4 writing and analysis, 1 review with the app's quizzes). This course is learned by **writing and discussing**, so keep a portfolio folder with every draft, the feedback you receive and your revisions.\n\n## Textbooks and references\n- Gerald Alred, Charles Brusaw and Walter Oliu, *Handbook of Technical Writing* (St. Martin's). A comprehensive reference for documents and style.\n- Mike Markel, *Technical Communication* (Bedford/St. Martin's). Chapters on audience, reports, proposals and presentations.\n- William Strunk and E. B. White, *The Elements of Style*; Joseph Williams, *Style: Lessons in Clarity and Grace*. For clear sentences.\n- Charles Harris, Michael Davis, Michael Pritchard and Michael Rabins, *Engineering Ethics: Concepts and Cases* (Cengage). The standard case-based text.\n- Mike Martin and Roland Schinzinger, *Ethics in Engineering*.\n- Nancy Leveson, *Engineering a Safer World* and \"An Investigation of the Therac-25 Accidents\" (Leveson and Turner, 1993). For safety and software case studies.\n- Diane Vaughan, *The Challenger Launch Decision* (normalisation of deviance).\n- The IEEE Code of Ethics, the NSPE Code of Ethics for Engineers and the ACM Code of Ethics (all free online); the IEEE Editorial Style Manual for references.\n- Your national engineering body's code of conduct (for example the Ghana Institution of Engineering) and the national standards authority's catalogue.\n\n## Free resources\n- Online Ethics Center (National Academy of Engineering) for case studies.\n- Purdue OWL for writing, citation and grammar.\n- Standards from IEEE Xplore (through your library), ISO and IEC previews, and RFCs from the IETF (free).\n- MIT OpenCourseWare technical and scientific writing courses (search the 21W subjects); Toastmasters-style practice for speaking.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Why communication and ethics matter (Ch 1.1, 1.2, 6.1).** Read two short case summaries (Challenger and Therac-25). Write a 300-word reflection: where did communication or ethics fail? Do: Assignment 1 Q1.\n\n**Week 2: Style, clarity and units (Ch 1.3, 1.4).** Do: Assignment 1 Q1, Q5. Habit: rewrite five sentences from your own previous coursework each day.\n\n**Week 3: Visuals and tables (Ch 1.5).** Do: Assignment 1 Q6. Redraw three figures from your lab reports to meet every rule in the checklist.\n\n**Week 4: Reports, abstracts and the IMRaD structure (Ch 2.1, 2.2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q2. Write a one-page summary of a lab you completed. Sit the **Class Test** at the end of the week.\n\n**Week 5: Requirements, proposals and professional correspondence (Ch 2.3, 2.4).** Do: Assignment 1 Q3, Q4.\n\n**Week 6: Citation, plagiarism and licences (Ch 3).** Do: Assignment 1 Q7 and Assignment 3 Q6. Practise IEEE referencing with ten sources.\n\n**Week 7: Presentations and teamwork (Ch 4).** Prepare and deliver a 5-minute talk on a project; record it and review. Write a team agreement.\n\n**Week 8: Standards: what, who and how (Ch 5.1 to 5.4).** Do: Assignment 2 Q1, Q2, Q3, Q8. Mock midterm: first half of the Final Exam.\n\n**Week 9: Risk, safety and reliability (Ch 5.5).** Do: Assignment 2 Q4 to Q7. Perform an FMEA on a small device you own.\n\n**Week 10: Ethical theories and codes (Ch 6.2 to 6.4).** Do: Assignment 3 Q1. Apply the seven-step method to two cases.\n\n**Week 11: Professional responsibilities: honesty, conflicts of interest, whistleblowing (Ch 7.1 to 7.3).** Do: Assignment 3 Q3, Q4, Q5.\n\n**Week 12: IP, privacy, security, sustainability, AI (Ch 7.4 to 7.6).** Do: Assignment 3 Q6, Q7, Q8.\n\n**Week 13: Case studies (Ch 8).** Do: Assignment 3 Q2, Q9. Write a full case analysis (about 1,200 words) of one case not covered in the notes (for example Toyota unintended acceleration or a local infrastructure failure), with references.\n\n**Week 14: Revision.** Redo the missed Class Test questions, then sit the **Final Exam** under exam conditions (3 hours, closed book except for a copy of the IEEE and NSPE codes).\n\n## Milestone checklist\n- [ ] I can state the audience, purpose and main message of any document before writing it.\n- [ ] I can write a clear abstract, a testable requirement and a professional email.\n- [ ] I can produce a correct graph or table with caption, units and references to it.\n- [ ] I can cite sources in IEEE style, and I can recognise plagiarism.\n- [ ] I can find, read and apply a standard, distinguishing normative and informative text.\n- [ ] I can estimate and reduce risk using a risk matrix, the hierarchy of controls and FMEA.\n- [ ] I can analyse a dilemma with several ethical theories and the codes, and defend a decision.\n- [ ] I can describe at least five engineering failures and their ethical and communication lessons.\n- [ ] I scored at least 70 percent on the Final Exam.\n\n## Study habits that work\n1. Write something every day, even 200 words, and let it rest overnight before revising.\n2. Read your draft aloud; stumbling shows where the sentence is unclear.\n3. Ask a friend from another discipline to read your document. If they cannot find the main point in 30 seconds, restructure.\n4. For ethics cases, always separate the facts from the judgements, and argue the other side's best case before deciding.\n5. Keep a list of ethics \"tests\" (publicity, role model, reversibility) and apply them each time.\n6. Join a professional society (IEEE student branch, your national engineering body) and read its code and its news.\n","test":"# Engineering Communication and Ethics: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 written problems (10 marks each).\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Which belongs in the abstract of a technical report?\nA) detailed raw data tables  B) the main quantitative results  C) the complete reference list  D) a long literature review\n\n**2.** Which sentence is in the preferred (active, concrete) style?\nA) \"A decision was reached that the circuit would be tested.\"  B) \"The team tested the circuit.\"  C) \"Testing of the circuit was performed by the team.\"  D) \"It is recommended that testing occurs.\"\n\n**3.** In a requirements document, \"shall\" indicates\nA) an optional feature  B) a mandatory requirement  C) a suggestion  D) a historical note\n\n**4.** In IEEE citation style, references are\nA) alphabetical by author and cited by year  B) numbered in the order of first citation  C) listed only as web links  D) unnecessary for figures\n\n**5.** Copying a paragraph from a paper without quotation marks or citation is\nA) paraphrasing  B) plagiarism  C) common knowledge  D) summarising\n\n**6.** The SI unit symbol for frequency is\nA) hz  B) HZ  C) Hz  D) hZ\n\n**7.** What should you do first before writing a technical document?\nA) choose the font  B) analyse the audience and purpose  C) write the conclusion  D) format the references\n\n**8.** The purpose of the Method section in a report is to\nA) summarise the conclusions  B) give enough detail for someone to reproduce the work  C) advertise the product  D) list the budget\n\n**9.** The first Fundamental Canon of the NSPE Code of Ethics is to\nA) maximise profit for the employer  B) hold paramount the safety, health and welfare of the public  C) obey every instruction from a manager  D) avoid disclosing any problems\n\n**10.** Which organisation is a primary international standards body?\nA) ISO  B) NASA  C) GitHub  D) NSF\n\n**11.** A simple risk measure is\nA) severity + cost  B) likelihood × severity  C) probability − severity  D) cost ÷ benefit\n\n**12.** The most effective level in the hierarchy of controls is\nA) personal protective equipment  B) warning signs  C) elimination of the hazard  D) training\n\n**13.** Utilitarianism judges an action by its\nA) intention only  B) consequences, favouring the greatest overall good  C) conformity to rules only  D) the actor's feelings\n\n**14.** An engineer who suspects a serious safety problem should first\nA) leak it to the press  B) verify the facts and raise the concern internally in writing  C) ignore it  D) delete the data\n\n**15.** A key cause of the Therac-25 accidents was\nA) a ship collision  B) software race conditions and the removal of hardware interlocks  C) a bridge failure  D) lack of electricity\n\n**16.** The Challenger disaster was caused by the failure of\nA) a main engine turbopump  B) an O-ring seal in a solid rocket booster joint in cold weather  C) the parachutes  D) the heat tiles\n\n**17.** In a standard, a \"NOTE\" is typically\nA) normative  B) informative  C) legally binding  D) a test requirement\n\n**18.** The GPL is an example of a\nA) permissive licence  B) copyleft licence  C) trademark  D) trade secret\n\n**19.** Which is a conflict of interest?\nA) a colleague's praise  B) accepting an expensive gift from a bidder you are evaluating  C) working overtime  D) using a company laptop for work\n\n**20.** 100 MB/s (megabytes per second) equals\nA) 100 Mb/s  B) 800 Mb/s  C) 12.5 Mb/s  D) 1000 Mb/s\n\n## Section B: Written problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (10 marks)** Rewrite each sentence to be concise, concrete and clear. (2.5 marks each)\n(a) \"It was observed by the technician that the oscillation of the output voltage was of a nature that was very large.\"\n(b) \"In the event that the battery voltage drops below the minimum threshold value, the system will make the determination to shut itself down.\"\n(c) \"The new motor is much more efficient.\"\n(d) \"Testing was performed at 20 and the result was 3.3.\"\n\n**B2. (10 marks)** A client wants a \"portable, fast, long-lasting weather station.\" Write five measurable, testable requirements (using \"shall\") that turn this brief into engineering requirements, and name a test for each.\n\n**B3. (10 marks)** A test engineer is told by a manager to sign a safety test report for a product even though one of the required tests (a 72-hour thermal soak) was shortened to 24 hours because of a deadline. (a) State the ethical issues [3]. (b) Evaluate two options using at least two ethical lenses and the codes [4]. (c) State what you recommend and write the first two sentences of your message to the manager [3].\n\n**B4. (10 marks)** A power supply design has three failure modes: output capacitor short (S 8, O 2, D 5); fan failure (S 5, O 6, D 2); firmware watchdog not working (S 9, O 2, D 7). (a) Compute the RPN for each and rank them [4]. (b) Recommend a priority and two actions [3]. (c) Classify as normative or informative: \"The unit shall shut down within 100 ms of an over-temperature condition.\" \"Designers should consider redundant sensors.\" \"The shutdown time may be tested using a thermal chamber.\" \"NOTE: Typical shutdown times are 50 ms.\" [3]\n\n---\n\n## Answer Key and Marking Guide\n\n### Section A\n1 **B** · 2 **B** · 3 **B** · 4 **B** · 5 **B** · 6 **C** · 7 **B** · 8 **B** · 9 **B** · 10 **A** · 11 **B** · 12 **C** · 13 **B** · 14 **B** · 15 **B** · 16 **B** · 17 **B** · 18 **B** · 19 **B** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons:\n1. The abstract summarises the key results with numbers.\n2. Active voice and a named actor.\n3. \"Shall\" is mandatory, \"should\" is a recommendation, \"may\" is permission.\n4. IEEE numbers references in order of first appearance.\n5. Verbatim copying without credit.\n6. Hz (the unit is named after Hertz, so the symbol is capitalised).\n7. Audience and purpose shape everything else.\n8. Reproducibility.\n9. The paramount duty to the public.\n10. ISO, the International Organization for Standardization.\n11. Risk is likelihood times severity.\n12. Eliminating the hazard is the strongest control.\n13. Consequentialism.\n14. Check the facts, document, and use internal channels first.\n15. Both were identified in the investigations.\n16. The O-ring seal.\n17. Notes are informative, not requirements.\n18. The GPL requires derivative works to be released under the same licence.\n19. A gift from a bidder could influence your judgement.\n20. 100 × 8 = 800 Mb/s.\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.** (a) \"The technician observed large oscillations in the output voltage.\" (Better: add the amplitude: \"...oscillations of 2 V peak to peak.\") (b) \"If the battery voltage falls below the minimum threshold, the system shuts down.\" (c) \"The new motor is 15 percent more efficient: 92 percent versus 80 percent.\" (any number-based rewrite; note that the original is vague). (d) \"We tested at 20 °C and measured 3.3 V.\" (units and clear actor). (1 mark for fixing padding or voice, 1.5 for precision.)\n\n**B2.** Model requirements:\n1. **Portability:** \"The station shall have a mass not exceeding 2.0 kg and fit within a 40 × 25 × 15 cm case.\" Test: measure mass and dimensions.\n2. **Speed (response):** \"The station shall update displayed temperature, humidity and wind speed at least once every 5 seconds.\" Test: log timestamps over 10 minutes.\n3. **Battery life:** \"The station shall operate for at least 30 days on one battery charge at 25 °C with a sampling interval of 1 minute.\" Test: run a discharge test (or accelerated test with calculated current).\n4. **Accuracy:** \"Temperature readings shall be accurate to ±0.5 °C over the range −10 °C to +50 °C.\" Test: compare with a calibrated reference in a thermal chamber.\n5. **Environmental:** \"The enclosure shall meet IP65 and operate in 0 to 95 percent relative humidity (non-condensing).\" Test: IP65 water-jet test, humidity chamber.\n(2 marks each: 1 for a measurable requirement with a number, unit and condition, 1 for a valid test. Deduct for compound or vague statements.)\n\n**B3.**\n(a) Issues: **public safety** (the shortened test may hide a failure that could harm users: overheating or fire); **honesty** (signing a report that implies the full test was done is deceptive); **loyalty and pressure** (the manager and deadline); **professional competence and responsibility** (the signature is a personal and professional assurance); **legal and regulatory** (misreporting certification tests can be fraud).\n(b) **Option 1: sign as instructed.** Utilitarian: possible short-term benefit (meeting the deadline) but risk of serious harm and recall costs; deontological: violates the duty of honesty; the codes (NSPE canons 1, 3 and 5; IEEE: be honest and realistic in stating claims; hold the public's safety paramount) forbid it. **Option 2: refuse to sign, report the deviation, and propose completing the 72-hour test (or accept a documented, risk-assessed concession).** Consequences: a short delay and an unhappy manager, but protects people and the company; it respects honesty and rights; a person of integrity would choose this; it passes the publicity test. (2 marks per option with two lenses.)\n(c) **Recommendation:** do not sign. Report that the report is inaccurate, request completing the full test or a documented, justified concession approved by the responsible safety authority, and escalate to quality or compliance if pressured. First two sentences: \"I cannot sign the safety report as written because the 72-hour thermal soak was run for only 24 hours, and the report must state what was actually done. I propose either completing the full test (it will take three more days) or documenting the deviation with a risk assessment for approval by Quality.\" (1 mark recommendation, 1 mark message content, 1 mark tone.)\n\n**B4.**\n(a) Step 1: RPN = S × O × D. Capacitor short = 8 × 2 × 5 = **80**.\nStep 2: fan failure = 5 × 6 × 2 = **60**.\nStep 3: watchdog not working = 9 × 2 × 7 = **126**.\nStep 4: ranking: **watchdog (126), capacitor short (80), fan failure (60)**.\n(b) Priority: the watchdog failure first. It has the highest RPN and the highest severity (9), and it is hard to detect (D = 7). Actions: add a built-in self-test and a hardware watchdog with an independent timer that is verified at start-up; include a test for watchdog failure in production tests; add monitoring that reports the failure. For the capacitor short, add a fuse and derate the capacitor.\n(c) \"The unit shall shut down within 100 ms...\" is **normative** (shall). \"Designers should consider redundant sensors\" is **normative recommendation** (should; strictly \"normative but not mandatory\"; accept \"recommended\" and note that it is not a requirement). \"The shutdown time may be tested using a thermal chamber\" is a **permission** (may; optional). \"NOTE: Typical shutdown times are 50 ms.\" is **informative**. (Award 1 mark for correctly identifying shall as mandatory, 1 for should/may handling, 1 for the NOTE.)\n"},mcq:[{"q":"Which belongs in the abstract of a technical report?","options":["detailed raw data tables","the main quantitative results","the complete reference list","a long literature review"],"answer":1,"explain":"The abstract summarises the key results with numbers.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which sentence is in the preferred (active, concrete) style?","options":["\"A decision was reached that the circuit would be tested.\"","\"The team tested the circuit.\"","\"Testing of the circuit was performed by the team.\"","\"It is recommended that testing occurs.\""],"answer":1,"explain":"Active voice and a named actor.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In a requirements document, \"shall\" indicates","options":["an optional feature","a mandatory requirement","a suggestion","a historical note"],"answer":1,"explain":"\"Shall\" is mandatory, \"should\" is a recommendation, \"may\" is permission.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In IEEE citation style, references are","options":["alphabetical by author and cited by year","numbered in the order of first citation","listed only as web links","unnecessary for figures"],"answer":1,"explain":"IEEE numbers references in order of first appearance.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Copying a paragraph from a paper without quotation marks or citation is","options":["paraphrasing","plagiarism","common knowledge","summarising"],"answer":1,"explain":"Verbatim copying without credit.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The SI unit symbol for frequency is","options":["hz","HZ","Hz","hZ"],"answer":2,"explain":"Hz (the unit is named after Hertz, so the symbol is capitalised).","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"What should you do first before writing a technical document?","options":["choose the font","analyse the audience and purpose","write the conclusion","format the references"],"answer":1,"explain":"Audience and purpose shape everything else.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The purpose of the Method section in a report is to","options":["summarise the conclusions","give enough detail for someone to reproduce the work","advertise the product","list the budget"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reproducibility.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The first Fundamental Canon of the NSPE Code of Ethics is to","options":["maximise profit for the employer","hold paramount the safety, health and welfare of the public","obey every instruction from a manager","avoid disclosing any problems"],"answer":1,"explain":"The paramount duty to the public.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which organisation is a primary international standards body?","options":["ISO","NASA","GitHub","NSF"],"answer":0,"explain":"ISO, the International Organization for Standardization.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A simple risk measure is","options":["severity + cost","likelihood × severity","probability − severity","cost ÷ benefit"],"answer":1,"explain":"Risk is likelihood times severity.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The most effective level in the hierarchy of controls is","options":["personal protective equipment","warning signs","elimination of the hazard","training"],"answer":2,"explain":"Eliminating the hazard is the strongest control.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Utilitarianism judges an action by its","options":["intention only","consequences, favouring the greatest overall good","conformity to rules only","the actor's feelings"],"answer":1,"explain":"Consequentialism.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"An engineer who suspects a serious safety problem should first","options":["leak it to the press","verify the facts and raise the concern internally in writing","ignore it","delete the data"],"answer":1,"explain":"Check the facts, document, and use internal channels first.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A key cause of the Therac-25 accidents was","options":["a ship collision","software race conditions and the removal of hardware interlocks","a bridge failure","lack of electricity"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both were identified in the investigations.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The Challenger disaster was caused by the failure of","options":["a main engine turbopump","an O-ring seal in a solid rocket booster joint in cold weather","the parachutes","the heat tiles"],"answer":1,"explain":"The O-ring seal.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In a standard, a \"NOTE\" is typically","options":["normative","informative","legally binding","a test requirement"],"answer":1,"explain":"Notes are informative, not requirements.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The GPL is an example of a","options":["permissive licence","copyleft licence","trademark","trade secret"],"answer":1,"explain":"The GPL requires derivative works to be released under the same licence.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which is a conflict of interest?","options":["a colleague's praise","accepting an expensive gift from a bidder you are evaluating","working overtime","using a company laptop for work"],"answer":1,"explain":"A gift from a bidder could influence your judgement.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"100 MB/s (megabytes per second) equals","options":["100 Mb/s","800 Mb/s","12.5 Mb/s","1000 Mb/s"],"answer":1,"explain":"100 × 8 = 800 Mb/s.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"You must cite a source when you","options":["use someone else's data, figures or ideas","state a universally known fact","use your own measurements","write the title"],"answer":0,"explain":"Credit anything that is not yours.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In most technical styles, a figure caption is placed","options":["above the figure","below the figure","on a separate page only","in the abstract"],"answer":1,"explain":"Figure captions below, table captions above.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The best subject line for an email reporting a test failure is","options":["\"Hi\"","\"Problem\"","\"Thermal test failed at 70 °C: decision needed on redesign\"","\"Please read!!!\""],"answer":2,"explain":"Informative, specific, and states the action needed.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"An executive summary is written mainly for","options":["decision makers who may read nothing else","laboratory technicians","the proofreader","the standards committee"],"answer":0,"explain":"Decision makers.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The symbol mA stands for","options":["megaamperes","milliamperes","microamperes","metres per ampere"],"answer":1,"explain":"The prefix m means milli, 10^-3.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"When you have a conflict of interest you should","options":["hide it","disclose it and, where necessary, recuse yourself","resign immediately","ask a friend to decide in secret"],"answer":1,"explain":"Transparency first.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Ariane 5 Flight 501 failure was caused by","options":["a fuel leak","an unhandled overflow in reused inertial reference software","a launch pad fire","a faulty parachute"],"answer":1,"explain":"A 64-bit floating-point value was converted to a 16-bit integer and overflowed.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Hyatt Regency walkway collapse was linked to","options":["a change in how the walkway hanger rods were connected, which increased the load on the box-beam connection","a hurricane","an earthquake","a lightning strike"],"answer":0,"explain":"The change from continuous rods to offset rods doubled the load on the box beam.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which is a basic functional-safety standard for electrical, electronic and programmable electronic systems?","options":["ISO 9001","IEC 61508","IEEE 754","RFC 791"],"answer":1,"explain":"IEC 61508.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A CE mark on a product indicates","options":["it is made in Europe","the manufacturer declares conformity with applicable EU requirements","it is the cheapest","it was tested by NASA"],"answer":1,"explain":"It is a declaration by the manufacturer, not a certification of quality.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In the hierarchy of controls, the least effective measure is","options":["elimination","substitution","engineering controls","PPE and warnings"],"answer":3,"explain":"Warnings and PPE depend on human behaviour.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Deontological ethics emphasises","options":["outcomes only","duties and rules, and respect for persons","profit","personal taste"],"answer":1,"explain":"Kant and the duty-based tradition.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Confidentiality may be overridden when","options":["the employer is embarrassed","there is a serious danger to the public that cannot otherwise be prevented","a colleague asks","a competitor offers money"],"answer":1,"explain":"The public interest overrides confidentiality only in serious cases.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A patent protects","options":["an idea expressed as text","an invention that is novel, non-obvious and useful","a brand name","a company slogan"],"answer":1,"explain":"Patents protect inventions.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which GDPR principle means collecting only the data you need?","options":["data minimisation","data maximisation","retention for ever","secret processing"],"answer":0,"explain":"Data minimisation.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Under the four-fifths rule, adverse impact is indicated when a group's selection rate is below what fraction of the highest group's rate?","options":["50 percent","60 percent","80 percent","95 percent"],"answer":2,"explain":"80 percent.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Under IEC 61508 (low-demand), SIL 2 corresponds to a PFDavg in the range","options":["10^-1 to 1","10^-2 to 10^-1","10^-3 to 10^-2","10^-4 to 10^-3"],"answer":2,"explain":"10^-3 to 10^-2.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"When you raise a safety concern, you should","options":["rely on a phone call only","keep written records and copies of evidence","delete the emails","tell only friends"],"answer":1,"explain":"Written records protect you and the public.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Passive voice is acceptable in technical writing when","options":["the actor is unknown or unimportant","you want to sound formal","you want longer sentences","never"],"answer":0,"explain":"For example \"The samples were heated\" when the actor is unimportant.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A key technical weakness identified in the 737 MAX accidents was that MCAS","options":["used two redundant sensors","relied on data from a single angle-of-attack sensor","had no software","was fully documented in pilot training"],"answer":1,"explain":"Single angle-of-attack sensor.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
